A||||3||rodmcneill||Help, won't start||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||06-06-2001||12:25 PM||||Driving home my GT last night I got a check engine light. I put my scanner on it and it came up a code 25(mat sensor) and a code 44(lean). It was running last night. This morning I replaced the mat sensor and then disconnected the + lead to the battery for 30 seconds so the codes would erased. With the battery hooked back up it will crank fast but will not start. The fuel pump is running, I have checked fuses. I am here on my own this morning so its hard to check for spark. It sounds like its running when I am cranking it but shuts off when I release the key. All the ignition parts are fairly new. I was planning to replace my pickup coil soon, I have one in the garage. Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rodmcneill||06-06-2001||01:29 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||01:41 PM||||First rule in debugging is to undo your last step - if you still have the old MAT sensor plug it in - dont install it, just plug it in.<P>  This may not make any sense - I dont think a bad mat sensor can stop the car from starting - but its a general rule to follow for debugging, worth the try.<P>  after that, spark at the plugs, smell of gas in the exhaust outlets, and compression are the lowest three branches of the debug tree - find out which one is missing and let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||06-06-2001||05:21 PM||||Got it running, YEAH! I had good spark and I could smell fuel. I pulled one sparkplug and the gap was a little wide(rapidfires with 30,000 miles) after regapping the second one I could tell she wanted to start. I did find bad corrosion on the coil wire terminal and on the coil terminal. I used to have a Grand Am that would have the same thing, I just had to clean it once in a while. Check yours! I will give my car a tune up this weekend. It looks like my problem was totally unrelated to the sensor replacement.||||reg||14
